course: myths
lesson: 37
title: Olympiad: how could you check it? ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The last olympiad puzzles of the course don't ask "true or false?" but the harder question: how could you find out, and which result would settle it?
explain: A claim with "never", "always" or "only" is the easiest to crack: one balanced egg on an ordinary day in May breaks "eggs only balance on the equinox".
explain: A claim with "often" needs counting: drop the toast twenty times and write each landing down, because memory keeps the butter-down ones and loses the rest.
explain: The cleverest tests hide the answer from the people who judge: if nobody knows which party drink had the sugar, nobody's hopes can colour what they see.
explain: And a test must be able to say "no": if Gran only counts the matches her lucky socks won, the socks can never fail, so the test proves nothing.
fact: With patience an egg can be balanced on its end on any day of the year; the "only on the equinox" story was spread by a newspaper in 1945.
fact: One true counter-example is enough to break a claim that says "never", "always" or "only".
fact: The Empire State Building is hit by lightning about 20 to 25 times a year, so lightning can strike the same place again and again.
fact: In one study, mothers were told their young sons had drunk sugar when the drink had none, and they still judged the boys more hyper; careful studies find that sugar does not make children hyperactive.
fact: A fair test changes only one thing, keeps everything else the same, and is repeated many times with the results written down.
fact: Every taste can be sensed wherever there are taste buds; the old tongue map came from a mistranslated German study of 1901.
fact: The Moon looks bigger near the horizon, but a coin held at arm's length, or a photo, shows it is the same size.
fact: Each hair of a polar bear's fur is colourless, and the skin underneath is black.
fact: Scientists find traces of paint on ancient Greek and Roman statues with special lights and microscopes.
fact: Scientists still bounce lasers off mirrors left on the Moon by the Apollo astronauts between 1969 and 1972.
fact: Paul Revere rode in 1775; the "The British are coming!" version comes mostly from a poem written in 1860.
fact: No Viking-age helmet with horns has ever been dug up; the Gjermundbu helmet found in Norway is a rounded cap.
fact: Stars twinkle because moving air bends their light; seen from space, above the air, they shine steadily.
fact: Headlines like "Chocolate makes you clever!" often come from a small study, sometimes on animals; ask who was tested, how many, and compared with what.
exercise: Mo says, "Eggs only balance on the equinox." Which result would prove him wrong? | An egg balanced on an ordinary day in May | An egg balanced on the equinox day itself, at noon | An egg that falls over on an ordinary day in May | Ten eggs that all fall over on the equinox
exercise: "Lightning never strikes the same place twice." Which fact breaks this claim straight away? | The Empire State Building gets hit about 20 times a year | Lightning is very hot and very bright | Thunder comes after the flash | Most storms happen in summer
exercise: Ella drops buttered toast off the table 20 times: 14 land butter down. What can she fairly say? | It often lands butter down, but not always | It always lands butter side down | It lands butter down exactly half the time | It never lands butter side down at all
exercise: Put the steps of a fair test in order: A write down results, B make a guess, C repeat it many times, D plan to change one thing. | B, D, C, A | D, B, C, A | B, C, D, A | A, B, D, C
exercise: Why should nobody know which party drink had the sugar in it? | So hopes can't colour what people see | So the sugar tastes much nicer to everyone | So the children drink more of it | So the test finishes much faster
exercise: Gran says her lucky socks win matches, but she only remembers the wins. What is wrong with her "test"? | It can never show the socks failing | She should wear them on both feet | She needs to wash the socks first | Socks only work in home matches, not away ones
exercise: How could you check the tongue map with a cotton bud and sugar water? | Dab it on the back: if it tastes sweet, the map is wrong | Dab it on the tip: if it tastes sweet, the map is right | Drink it all at once and say which part tasted it | Dab it on your lips and then wait for one whole minute
exercise: How could you check whether the Moon really is bigger near the horizon? | Hold a coin at arm's length, low and high | Look at it through a long cardboard tube at night | Ask your friends whether it looks big to them | Wait for a full moon and stare
exercise: How could you check what colour a polar bear's hair really is? | Look at one hair closely | Look at a photo from far away | Count the bears in the zoo | Ask a polar bear keeper's friend
exercise: How do scientists know ancient statues were once brightly painted? | Special lights find traces of paint | Old films show them being painted | The marble changes colour when it rains | The statues all have their names on them
exercise: Which is the strongest evidence that astronauts really reached the Moon? | Lasers still bounce off the mirrors they left there | A poster of the landing in a museum shop | A film made about it many years later | A friend who says his grandad watched it
exercise: How many years after Paul Revere's ride was the famous poem written? | 85 | 75 | 185 | 58
exercise: Which evidence would best show what real Viking helmets looked like? | Helmets dug up from the Viking age | An opera costume made in 1876, long after | A cartoon about Vikings on TV | A Viking statue in a theme park
exercise: How could you check that stars twinkle because of our air? | Ask whether they twinkle when seen from space | Count how many stars you can see tonight | Look at them through dark sunglasses at midnight | Check whether they twinkle in the winter
exercise: A headline says "Chocolate makes you clever!" What should you ask first? | Who was tested, how many, and compared with what? | Which shop sells the cheapest chocolate bars? | How many bars should I eat to be really clever? | Is milk chocolate cleverer than dark chocolate?
exercise: Snowflakes: "No two are ever alike." What would break the "ever"? | Two tiny simple crystals that match exactly | Two big snowflakes that look very different | A snowflake that melts on your glove | A photo of one very pretty snowflake
test: Ruby, from Glasgow: "Ma da says sugar makes us mental at parties, ken. Proven." Which test would really check it, aye? | Same-tasting drinks, half with sugar, nobody told which | Give all the kids sugar and see if they go wild | Ask the parents if their kids seemed hyper | Watch one party and count who's running about
test: "Lola put one pot by the radio in the sun and one in a dark cupboard, radio pot won, so music works, sorted" Fix her test. | Same sunny spot for both, radio for one | Put both pots in the cupboard with the radio | Use louder music and a bigger pot | Give the radio pot more water as well
test: Your mate: "I've balanced an egg like a billion times on the equinox, so it ONLY works then." What's the hole in his proof? | He never tried on any other day | A billion tries is still not quite enough | Eggs really do stand only on the equinox | He should have used hard-boiled eggs
test: "my brother's mate's dad saw a horned Viking helmet in a museum, no cap" How strong is that? | Weak: it can't be checked, and none has been dug up | Strong: a dad would never get a museum wrong | Strong: three people all agree on it | Weak: museums never have Viking things
test: "We dropped the toast twice and it landed butter down both times, so it's ALWAYS butter down." What's the problem? | Two drops are far too few | The toast was the wrong kind of bread | Butter makes toast land the right way up | Nothing, two drops prove it for ever
test: Gran, bless her: "My socks have won us heaps of matches, I've lost count, love." What would make her socks test fair? | Count wins and losses with the socks and without | Wear the socks to even more of the team's matches | Ask the team if they like the socks | Buy a new pair and count only those wins
test: "teacher said the poem about Paul Revere proves what he shouted lol" What is the best reply? | The poem came 85 years later, so it's a story, not evidence | A poem is always the best kind of proof | Paul Revere wrote the poem himself at the time | Poems can only be written about true things
test: "chain msg says it KNOWS if u didn't share it. bare scary bruv" How could you check? | Lots never share it and nothing happens | Share it ten times and see what happens to you | Ask the sender if the message knows | Turn your phone off for a week
test: Uncle Dev: "That headline about chocolate's slightly overcooked." The study was on 12 mice. What does he really mean? | It claims far more than the study shows | The chocolate in the study was a bit burnt | The headline is spot on and fair | Twelve mice prove it for every child
test: Which result could show the tongue map is WRONG? | Sugar tastes sweet at the back | Sugar on the tip of the tongue tastes sweet | Salt on the side of the tongue tastes salty | Lemon on the side of the tongue tastes sour
test: Jake measures the big orange Moon at the horizon with a coin at arm's length, then again at midnight. What will he find? | The coin covers it the same both times | It's twice as big at the horizon, low down | It's twice as big at midnight, high up | The coin won't cover it at either time
exam: Identify the single observation sufficient to refute the assertion that eggs may be balanced only upon the equinox. | An egg balanced upon an ordinary day | An egg balanced upon the equinox | Numerous eggs falling upon the equinox | A newspaper report of 1945
exam: Determine the purpose of concealing from all participants which beverage contains sugar. | To prevent expectation from influencing what is observed | To improve the flavour of the beverages | To reduce the duration of the experiment | To ensure that every child consumes sugar
exam: It is given that a ride took place in 1775 and a poem describing it was written in 1860. Calculate the interval between them. | Eighty-five years | Seventy-five years | Sixty-five years | One hundred and eighty-five years
exam: Select the most reliable evidence concerning the appearance of Viking helmets. | Helmets excavated from the Viking age | Costumes designed for an opera | Illustrations in modern comic books and films | Accounts passed on by word of mouth
